## Apache APISIX for Kubernetes

Apache APISIX is a dynamic, real-time, high-performance API gateway.

APISIX provides rich traffic management features such as load balancing, dynamic upstream, canary release, circuit breaking, authentication, observability, and more.

You can use Apache APISIX to handle traditional north-south traffic, as well as east-west traffic between services. It can also be used as a [k8s ingress controller](https://github.com/apache/apisix-ingress-controller/).

This chart bootstraps all the components needed to run Apache APISIX on a Kubernetes Cluster using [Helm](https://helm.sh).

## Prerequisites

* Kubernetes v1.14+
* Helm v3+

## Install

To install the chart with the release name `my-apisix`:

```sh
helm repo add apisix https://apache.github.io/apisix-helm-chart
helm repo update

helm install [RELEASE_NAME] apisix/apisix --namespace ingress-apisix --create-namespace
```

## Uninstall

 To uninstall/delete a Helm release `my-apisix`:

 ```sh
helm delete [RELEASE_NAME] --namespace ingress-apisix
 ```

The command removes all the Kubernetes components associated with the chart and deletes the release.

## Configure the wait-etcd init container

When `etcd.enabled` is true, `initContainer.resources` and
`initContainer.securityContext` configure the built-in `wait-etcd` container for
both Deployment and DaemonSet workloads. They are independent of the top-level
`resources` and `securityContext`, which configure the APISIX container.

Both options default to `{}` and are omitted from the rendered container when
empty. Applicable `podSecurityContext` settings still apply to `wait-etcd`.
For example:

```yaml
initContainer:
  resources:
    requests:
      cpu: 10m
      memory: 16Mi
    limits:
      cpu: 100m
      memory: 32Mi
  securityContext:
    allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
    capabilities:
      drop:
        - ALL
    readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
    runAsNonRoot: true
    runAsUser: 1000
```

Adjust resource values for your environment. The default BusyBox image runs as
root, so `runAsNonRoot: true` also requires a non-zero UID supplied through
`runAsUser`, `podSecurityContext`, or an admission policy. On platforms such as
OpenShift that assign a UID, omit the explicit `runAsUser` value.

These options do not configure `extraInitContainers` and have no effect when
`etcd.enabled` is false.

## Connect to external etcd with server-only TLS

For external etcd, use `https://` endpoints in `externalEtcd.host` and set
`etcd.auth.tls.enabled: true` to configure TLS verification and SNI. Client
certificates are optional: leave `existingSecret`, `certFilename` and
`certKeyFilename` empty when etcd does not require a client certificate.

For example, to connect to an IP endpoint using a CA stored in a Secret:

```yaml
apisix:
  ssl:
    enabled: true
    existingCASecret: etcd-ca
    certCAFilename: ca.crt
etcd:
  enabled: false
  auth:
    tls:
      enabled: true
      existingSecret: ""
      certFilename: ""
      certKeyFilename: ""
      verify: true
      sni: etcd.example.test
externalEtcd:
  host:
    - https://192.0.2.10:2379
  user: ""  # This example assumes etcd username/password authentication is disabled.
```

Replace the endpoint, Secret name, CA key and SNI with your actual values. The CA
Secret must be in the same namespace as APISIX. If etcd username/password
authentication is enabled, configure `externalEtcd.user` and its password or
password Secret as usual.

The SNI must be a DNS name covered by every endpoint's server certificate. It
does not change the connection address or require DNS resolution when the
endpoints are IP addresses. An empty SNI is omitted from the APISIX configuration
so the client can use the endpoint hostname.

For mTLS, keep `etcd.auth.tls.enabled: true` and set `existingSecret`,
`certFilename` and `certKeyFilename` under `etcd.auth.tls`. The certificate and key
must be configured together. When TLS is enabled, a non-empty `existingSecret`
is mounted at `/etcd-ssl`, including when certificate paths are supplied through
`apisix.fullCustomConfig`. The CA Secret above is configured separately.

With `etcd.auth.tls.enabled: false`, the APISIX etcd TLS settings and
client-certificate mount are omitted as before. This flag does not rewrite
external endpoint URLs; use `http://` endpoints for HTTP connections.

## Data encryption

APISIX encrypts the private keys of SSL objects and of upstream client
certificates (`tls.client_key`), and sensitive plugin fields such as the
credentials of authentication plugins, before it stores them in etcd. It
encrypts with the first key of `apisix.dataEncryption.keyring` and tries every
key when it decrypts. Each key is 16 or 32 bytes long (AES-128 or AES-256), for
example 16 or 32 ASCII letters, and is used as its own IV. With
`apisix.dataEncryption.enableEncryptFields: false`, plugin fields are stored in
plain text; private keys are still encrypted with the keyring.

When no keyring is set, APISIX uses its built-in keyring, `qeddd145sfvddff3`
and `edd1c9f0985e76a2`. These keys are published in the APISIX source code, so
anyone who can read etcd can decrypt the data. Set your own keyring, preferably
in an existing Secret: keys set in the values are stored in plain text in the
APISIX ConfigMap, which the Kubernetes `view` role can read, and in every
revision of the Helm release. For a release that stores no encrypted data yet:

```shell
kubectl create secret generic apisix-keyring \
  --from-literal=key1="$(LC_ALL=C tr -dc 'A-Za-z' </dev/urandom | head -c 16)"
```

```yaml
apisix:
  dataEncryption:
    secretName: apisix-keyring
    secretKeys:
      - key1
```

The chart passes each key to APISIX as an environment variable,
`APISIX_DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY_1` for the first one and so on, and APISIX reads
them when a pod starts: after you change the keys in the Secret, restart the
APISIX pods. With `apisix.fullCustomConfig`, the chart does not render
`data_encryption`; refer to these variables in your own configuration instead.
The chart renders `apisix.fullCustomConfig.config` as a template, so escape the
braces:

```yaml
apisix:
  dataEncryption:
    secretName: apisix-keyring
  fullCustomConfig:
    enabled: true
    config:
      apisix:
        data_encryption:
          keyring:
            - {{ `'${{ "{{" }}APISIX_DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY_1{{ "}}" }}'` }}
```

Until [apache/apisix#14178](https://github.com/apache/apisix/issues/14178) is
fixed in the APISIX version you run, use keys that do not look like a number,
such as keys made of letters: APISIX turns a value from an environment variable
that looks like a number into a number, whether it is quoted or not, and then
refuses to start.

The chart fails to render when the first key is one of the built-in keys, or
when a key is not 16 or 32 bytes long. It cannot check keys in a Secret: APISIX
ignores a key of another length in bytes, and stores the data unencrypted when no
key is left.

Use the same keyring for every release that reads the same etcd, for example
the control plane and the data plane of a decoupled deployment. Encryption only
applies when APISIX reads its configuration from etcd.

### Changing the keyring of an existing release

Data can only be decrypted with the key that encrypted it, and three more
things make a change of the keyring delicate:

- APISIX tries the keys in order, and about one value in 256 passes the check
  of AES-CBC decryption with a wrong key. APISIX then uses garbage for that
  value: the gateway rejects the credential, APISIX cannot load such an SSL
  object, and a GET returns the garbage, which a PUT would store for good.
- A pod that does not have a key cannot read what other pods encrypt with it.
- The Admin API does not return SSL private keys, so they cannot be read back
  and saved again.

To move an existing release off the built-in keyring, or to rotate your own
key:

1. Back up etcd, for example with `etcdctl snapshot save`.
2. While the release still runs with its current keyring, GET each resource
   that configures a plugin with encrypted fields, for example each consumer and
   its credentials, and keep the bodies. Remove their `create_time` and
   `update_time` fields, which a PUT rejects. The bodies hold the secrets in
   plain text, so keep them safe and delete them at the end. For a consumer:

   ```shell
   curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:9180/apisix/admin/consumers/$CONSUMER" -H "X-API-KEY: $ADMIN_KEY" |
     jq '.value | del(.create_time, .update_time)' >"consumer-$CONSUMER.json"
   ```

3. Upgrade with the new key first and the current keys after it:

   ```yaml
   apisix:
     dataEncryption:
       keyring:
         - "<new key>"
         - qeddd145sfvddff3
         - edd1c9f0985e76a2
   ```

   With a Secret, put the current keys in it after the new one:

   ```shell
   kubectl create secret generic apisix-keyring \
     --from-literal=key1="<new key>" \
     --from-literal=key2=qeddd145sfvddff3 \
     --from-literal=key3=edd1c9f0985e76a2
   ```

   ```yaml
   apisix:
     dataEncryption:
       secretName: apisix-keyring
       secretKeys: [key1, key2, key3]
   ```

   Until every APISIX pod runs with the new keyring, avoid changes through the
   Admin API, including those of the ingress controller. In a decoupled
   deployment, upgrade the data planes before the control plane. When you rotate
   your own key, you can avoid that pause: first add the new key after the
   current one, and move it to the front in a second upgrade.

4. PUT the bodies from step 2, not the result of a new GET:

   ```shell
   curl -s -X PUT http://127.0.0.1:9180/apisix/admin/consumers -H "X-API-KEY: $ADMIN_KEY" -d @"consumer-$CONSUMER.json"
   ```

   Then upload each SSL object, and each upstream, route and service that sets
   `tls.client_key`, again with its PEM key, for example from the Kubernetes
   Secret it came from: the Admin API cannot re-encrypt them from what it stores.
   Until this step is done, the gateway can reject some of the credentials stored
   before step 3, and APISIX cannot load about one SSL object in 256 (its log
   says `failed to parse key`), so TLS handshakes for that object's SNIs fail.
   Private keys that are still encrypted with the built-in keys can be read by
   anyone who can read etcd.

5. You can keep the old keys after the new one; that does not weaken the new
   key. Remove them only after step 4 is done for every resource: APISIX cannot
   decrypt anything that is still encrypted with a key that is no longer in the
   keyring.

A rollback to a revision from before the change, with `helm rollback` or by
reverting the values, removes the new key, and APISIX can no longer read what was
encrypted with it. Undo other changes with an upgrade that keeps the current
keyring.

